Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) may be more likely to be giants, but Vmag alone is not definitive since it depends on distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants tend to be luminous and can be seen at larger distances, so smaller parallax may indicate giants.
- e_Plx (parallax error): Larger errors reduce confidence in distance and absolute magnitude estimates, but do not directly indicate giant status.
- B-V (color index): Giants have characteristic color indices depending on their spectral type; generally, giants can have higher B-V (redder) than dwarfs of the same spectral class.
- SpType (spectral type): Spectral types with luminosity class III or IV (e.g., G8III, G7III) indicate giants or subgiants; V indicates main sequence (dwarf) stars.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitudes (brighter intrinsically) than dwarfs of the same spectral type; higher Amag values (fainter) correspond to dwarfs.

Step 2.  
10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, F0V, K5V, B8V, B9V]  
- SpType ends with "V" (main sequence stars)  
- Amag >= 15  
- Amag > 14  
- B-V <= 0.7  
- B-V < 0.8  
- Plx >= 5  
- Plx > 8  
- Vmag > 6  
- Vmag >= 7  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [G8III, G7III, B9III/IV, K3III]  
- SpType contains "III" or "IV" (giant or subgiant luminosity classes)  
- Amag < 16  
- Amag <= 16  
- B-V > 0.8  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx < 5  
- Plx <= 5  
- Vmag < 6  
- Vmag <= 5  

These conditions are derived from the spectral type indicating luminosity class, absolute magnitude ranges, color index, and parallax consistent with the examples and general stellar classification knowledge.

