Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): This measures how bright a star appears from Earth but depends on distance. Alone, it is not a reliable indicator of whether a star is a giant. Giants can be bright or faint depending on distance.
- Plx (parallax): Indicates distance; larger parallax means closer star. Giants tend to be more luminous and thus can be seen farther away at brighter apparent magnitudes.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; high error may reduce confidence in distance and absolute magnitude calculations.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature/color. Giants often have distinctive B-V ranges (e.g., redder for K and M giants) compared to dwarfs.
- SpType (spectral type): Contains luminosity class (e.g., V for main sequence/dwarf, III for giant). This is a very direct indicator of whether the star is a giant.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (more negative) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.

